Fillets: a corner whose cut crosses the line of the face at the end's seam
past the seam's end at the corner's vertex runs the seam on to the cut
(mitcad#343).

The case: three roundings of radius 0.5 along the curves where three
bores meet, each ending on a rod of radius 2.5 that stands in their
middle; the rod's cylinder face runs all round, its seam from its bottom
edge up to the vertex where two of the bores' curves on the rod meet, the
end vertex of one rounding. At that end the face at the end grows up to
the rounding: the corner's cut (the blend's intersection with the rod)
lies beyond the face's old boundary and crosses the seam's line 0.31 past
the seam's end. PerformOneCorner splits the cut where it crosses the seam
only where the crossing lies on the seam's edge (or at one of its
vertices); here the cut was left whole across the line, the seam ended at
the vertex the rounding removes, and the rod's face came out with a wire
not closed (its parameters jumping by the period).

Where the seam ends at the corner's vertex and the cut's curve on the face
crosses the seam's line beyond that end (on the face's surface, within the
tolerances), the cut is now split there as on the seam, and the seam runs
on along its line from the vertex to the crossing: a curve of the data
structure on the face's iso-line through the seam (its surface's own
parametrization, as the seam's curve, a B-spline here, cannot be
prolonged), on both sides of the seam, each the way the seam runs there,
from the vertex to the cut's point. The seam itself stays whole. On the
design's replayed body the rod's face has the file's area (412.4848
against 412.4839 mm2) and every new face lies within 6e-4 of the file's.

Upstream: new.
